  {
    "page": 13,
    "shapes": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Before you scream that these are two many to resemble human knowledge?",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          365125,
          10515600,
          1325563
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "How do you know that chickens cackle, but that ducks quack?\nThis kind of knowledge is in the synapses between the neurons of your brain?\nHow many synapses do you have?\nIn or around 150 Trillion!",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          1825625,
          10515600,
          4351338
        ]
      }
    ],
    "speakerNotes": ""
  },
  {
    "page": 14,
    "shapes": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Still you may think: What????? Knowledge is Completion????? Come On!",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          365125,
          10515600,
          1325563
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "This is so nonsensical that Yann LeCun just raised a billion dollars out of this not making any sense.\nBut why don’t you be the judge?\nIf you are interacting with an AI system, do you think you are interacting with a system whose notion of knowledge is totally different from your own?\nPersonally, for me, the answer is that whatever its notion of knowledge is, its not totally different from mine.\nAnother term for Completion is Prediction. And that word sits better with people. We do predict, expect…\n",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          1825625,
          10515600,
          4351338
        ]
      }
    ],
    "speakerNotes": ""
  },
  {
    "page": 15,
    "shapes": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Now we will go to \u000bLearning",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          365125,
          10515600,
          1325563
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One of the most central issues of AI is how it learns\nIt learns by making mistakes\nWe may not be that different…\nThis is from a reddit motivational page\nWe will now look at Stochastic Gradient Descent, the main algorithm of learning in AI!\nSounds intimidating, but you can handle it, just stay focused\nWe will learn with a very very easy example: Averaging",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          1825625,
          4933950,
          4351338
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "https://www.reddit.com/r/GetMotivated/comments/1072qzr/image_we_cant_avoid_making_mistakes_but_we_can/",
        "bounds": [
          5942405,
          5530632,
          6100762,
          646331
        ]
      }
    ],
    "speakerNotes": ""
  },
  {
    "page": 16,
    "shapes": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "What’s the big deal? Averaging is so easy",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          365125,
          10515600,
          1325563
        ]
      }
    ],
    "speakerNotes": ""
  },
  {
    "page": 17,
    "shapes": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          365126,
          10512972,
          315912
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "What’s the average, the essence, of: [78,67,98,85,76,68,98,72]?\n1) Pick any random number x as the estimate of average, and we will improve it by observation. We are at time 0 in the algorithm. Let’s say the random number we pick is 130: x[0] = 130.\n2) Randomly pick a number d from the data, so we can see how far the data is from the estimate, as the average should be close to the data. Let’s say we pick d[0] = 98.\n3) x[0] = 130 and d[0] = 98. The mistake we’ve made is Loss = 130 – 98 = 32.\n4) Now let’s improve the estimate by reducing the discrepancy mistake = Loss by subtracting a bit of the Loss, let’s say 10% from the current estimate.\nx[1] = x[0] -  10% of 32\nx[1] = 130 – 3.2 = 126.8 \n5) Repeat: Now pick another number d from the data to improve x from x[1] to x[2].\nWhat have we done? 1) Made an intial estimate/guess, 2) Used data to improve the estimate. \nStochastic (Random) Gradient (Loss-related) Descent (reduce)\n",
        "bounds": [
          838200,
          817418,
          10591800,
          5832764
        ]
      }
    ],
    "speakerNotes": ""
  },
  {
    "page": 18,
